<h4 style="margin: 0 0 5px 0; font-size: 1.2rem;">The Social Evaluation of Language</h4>
<p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.5; white-space: normal;">
How do children evaluate speakers based on the languages they speak or the accents they have? My core programmatic research treats bilingualism as a social category that triggers inferences about competence. While bilingualism is publicly celebrated as an asset, my work investigates the "double standard" where not all bilinguals benefit equally from this prestige.<br><br>Using forced-choice paradigms and behavioral measures, this work establishes that children generally apply a "more languages = more status" rule, whereas adults hold competing associations of prestige and foreignness. I am currently expanding this line of work in my dissertation to test the mechanisms behind these status attributions—specifically exploring selective trust, cultural learning, and boundary conditions like perceived phenotype. My theoretical work synthesizes how accents function as cues to trust, disentangling theories of social identity from processing fluency.
</p>
